\title{Contraction Calculus for Crystal Skeletons:\\Reachability, Character Decomposition, and Iterated Quotients}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 17, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a self-contained calculus for hierarchical contraction of finite weighted directed graphs, motivated by crystal skeletons, quasicrystal decompositions, Young quasisymmetric Schur functions, and the order structures arising after successive contractions. A contraction induced by a map $q:V\to Q$ places an edge between quotient labels whenever an original edge has endpoints in the corresponding fibers. Successive contractions agree with contraction by the composite quotient map. Under directed fiber connectivity---every ordered pair in one fiber is joined by a directed path---quotient reachability is equivalent to original reachability, reachability depends only on fibers, and antisymmetry descends. For finite weighted graphs, fiber characters partition the total character and aggregate associatively through repeated contractions. We provide constructive algorithms, examples, failure modes, and the interface with the proposed passage from crystals through Young-quasisymmetric tiles toward Bruhat-type quotients and Stanley symmetric functions.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

A crystal graph packages representation-theoretic information into a directed, often edge-colored combinatorial object. Its vertices carry weights, and the sum of their weight monomials forms the crystal character. In type $A$, the character of a connected highest-weight crystal is a Schur polynomial. A finer partition into quasicrystals exposes Gessel fundamental quasisymmetric functions. Contracting those pieces creates a crystal skeleton; grouping skeleton vertices again produces larger tiles expected to carry Young quasisymmetric Schur characters. A final contraction can reveal an order such as Bruhat order.

This hierarchy raises two independent questions. First, which directed paths survive compression? Second, how do vertex weights combine into component and global characters? Fine paths always descend to a quotient, but a coarse path can splice incompatible representatives: it may enter a fiber at one vertex and leave through another that cannot be reached. We isolate directed fiber connectivity as a sufficient condition ruling out this defect. On the enumerative side, fibers partition the vertex set, and finite sums regroup associatively.

Our principal conclusions are that contraction is associative, directed fiber connectivity gives exact path lifting and reachability preservation, antisymmetry descends, and character aggregation is associative through arbitrary finite hierarchies. The results separate universal quotient mechanics from the specialized tableau combinatorics required to identify quasicrystal characters, Young quasisymmetric Schur characters, Bruhat covers, and Stanley symmetric functions.

\section{Directed graphs and contraction}

Let $V$ be a set and $E\subseteq V\times V$ a directed edge relation. Write $x\to_Ey$ when $(x,y)\in E$. A directed path from $x$ to $y$ is a finite sequence $x=x_0,x_1,\ldots,x_k=y$ with $x_i\to_E x_{i+1}$ for every $i<k$. Paths of length zero are allowed. We write $x\reach{E}y$ for reachability.

\begin{definition}[Contraction]
Given a map $q:V\to Q$, the contracted relation $E/q$ on $Q$ is defined by
\[
a\to_{E/q}b \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\exists x,y\in V:\ q(x)=a,\ q(y)=b,\ x\to_Ey.
\]
Unused labels are permitted, and loops are retained.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Edge descent]
If $x\to_Ey$, then $q(x)\to_{E/q}q(y)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The original endpoints themselves witness the contracted edge.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Path descent]
If $x\reach{E}y$, then $q(x)\reach{E/q}q(y)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Map every vertex of a path through $q$ and apply edge descent to each successive pair. The path of length zero maps to a path of length zero.
\end{proof}

Thus contraction cannot lose reachability between images. Additional structure is needed to ensure that it does not create spurious reachability.

\section{Associativity of graph contraction}

Let $q:V\to Q$ and $r:Q\to S$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-stage contraction identity]
The relation obtained by contracting first along $q$ and then along $r$ equals contraction along the composite map:
\[
(E/q)/r=E/(r\circ q).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $s\to_{(E/q)/r}t$, choose $a,b\in Q$ with $r(a)=s$, $r(b)=t$, and $a\to_{E/q}b$. Choose $x,y\in V$ witnessing the latter edge. Then $(r\circ q)(x)=s$, $(r\circ q)(y)=t$, and $x\to_Ey$, so $x,y$ witness an edge in $E/(r\circ q)$. Conversely, if $x,y$ witness an edge under the composite map, then $q(x),q(y)$ witness the same edge after the two separate contractions.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Three-stage contraction identity]
For $t:S\to T$,
\[
((E/q)/r)/t=E/(t\circ r\circ q).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the two-stage identity twice and use associativity of function composition.
\end{proof}

By induction, every finite hierarchy has a canonical final edge relation, independent of parenthesization.

\section{Directed fiber connectivity and lifting}

\begin{definition}[Directed fiber connectivity]
The map $q:V\to Q$ has directed-connected fibers with respect to $E$ if
\[
q(x)=q(y)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad x\reach{E}y
\]
for every ordered pair $x,y\in V$.
\end{definition}

This is stronger than connectivity after forgetting edge directions. Each nonempty fiber lies within a strongly connected component. The strength is natural: a lifted quotient path may have to move from an arbitrary entry representative to a different exit representative.

\begin{lemma}[Lifting one edge from a prescribed representative]
Assume directed fiber connectivity. If $q(x)\to_{E/q}b$, then there is $y\in V$ such that $q(y)=b$ and $x\reach{E}y$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Choose $u,v$ witnessing the quotient edge, so $q(u)=q(x)$, $q(v)=b$, and $u\to_Ev$. Fiber connectivity gives $x\reach{E}u$. Append $u\to_Ev$ and take $y=v$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Path lifting]
Assume directed fiber connectivity. If $a\reach{E/q}b$ and $x\in V$ satisfies $q(x)=a$, then there exists $y\in V$ with $q(y)=b$ and $x\reach{E}y$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on the quotient path. A zero-length path lifts with $y=x$. For a longer path, lift its initial segment and apply the preceding lemma to its last edge. Concatenate the resulting paths.
\end{proof}

\section{Exact reachability and order}

\begin{theorem}[Exact Reachability Theorem]
If $q$ has directed-connected fibers, then for all $x,y\in V$,
\[
q(x)\reach{E/q}q(y) \quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\reach{E}y.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For the forward direction, path lifting produces $z$ with $q(z)=q(y)$ and $x\reach{E}z$. Fiber connectivity gives $z\reach{E}y$, and concatenation yields $x\reach{E}y$. The reverse direction is path descent.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fiber invariance]
If $q(x)=q(x')$ and $q(y)=q(y')$, then
\[
x\reach{E}y\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x'\reach{E}y'.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both statements are equivalent to reachability between the common source and target labels in $E/q$.
\end{proof}

Now let $q:V\to Q$ and $r:Q\to S$. Assume the $q$-fibers are directed-connected for $E$ and the $r$-fibers are directed-connected for $E/q$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-Stage Reachability Theorem]
For all $x,y\in V$,
\[
r(q(x))\reach{(E/q)/r}r(q(y))
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad x\reach{E}y.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply the Exact Reachability Theorem to $r$ on the intermediate graph and then to $q$ on the original graph.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Antisymmetry after contraction]
Assume directed fiber connectivity and suppose original reachability is antisymmetric. If $q(x)\reach{E/q}q(y)$ and $q(y)\reach{E/q}q(x)$, then $q(x)=q(y)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Exact reachability gives $x\reach{E}y$ and $y\reach{E}x$. Antisymmetry gives $x=y$, hence equality of their images.
\end{proof}

This is the general order-theoretic safeguard needed when a final skeleton is to be identified with a Bruhat-type order.

\section{Fiber characters}

Let $V$ be finite, let $A$ be a commutative additive monoid with identity $0$, and assign each vertex a weight $w(x)\in A$. This setting includes numerical weights, vectors, polynomials viewed additively, and finite formal generating functions.

\begin{definition}[Total and fiber characters]
The total character is
\[
\Ch(V,w)=\sum_{x\in V}w(x).
\]
For $q:V\to Q$ and $a\in Q$, the fiber character is
\[
\Ch_q(a)=\sum_{\substack{x\in V\\q(x)=a}}w(x).
\]
An empty fiber has character $0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Character partition theorem]
If $Q$ is finite, then
\[
\sum_{a\in Q}\Ch_q(a)=\Ch(V,w).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Expand the left side as a double sum. For each $x$, exactly the summand indexed by $q(x)$ contributes $w(x)$; every other label contributes zero. Reorder the finite sum.
\end{proof}

No connectivity assumption appears here. Character decomposition is a partition identity rather than a path-lifting claim.

\begin{theorem}[Fiber-Character Associativity Theorem]
Let $q:V\to Q$ and $r:Q\to S$. For every $s\in S$,
\[
\Ch_{r\circ q}(s)
=
\sum_{\substack{a\in Q\\r(a)=s}}\Ch_q(a).
\]
Equivalently,
\[
\sum_{\substack{x\in V\\r(q(x))=s}}w(x)
=
\sum_{\substack{a\in Q\\r(a)=s}}
\sum_{\substack{x\in V\\q(x)=a}}w(x).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The fibers $q^{-1}(a)$ with $r(a)=s$ are a disjoint partition of $(r\circ q)^{-1}(s)$. Regroup the finite sum by the intermediate label $a=q(x)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Total character from final tiles]
If $S$ is finite, then
\[
\sum_{s\in S}\sum_{\substack{a\in Q\\r(a)=s}}\Ch_q(a)
=
\sum_{x\in V}w(x).
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Sum the associativity identity over $s$ and apply the character partition theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Three-level character associativity]
For $t:S\to T$ and every $c\in T$,
\[
\Ch_{t\circ r\circ q}(c)
=
\sum_{\substack{s\in S\\t(s)=c}}
\sum_{\substack{a\in Q\\r(a)=s}}\Ch_q(a).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply fiber-character associativity first to $(r\circ q,t)$ and then to $(q,r)$ inside each summand.
\end{proof}

The result extends inductively to every finite hierarchy. Neither final component characters nor the total character depend on parenthesization.

\section{Constructive algorithms}

For an edge-list representation, the contracted graph is built by scanning every edge $(x,y)$ and inserting $(q(x),q(y))$ into a set. With constant-time labels and hashing, this takes expected time $O(|E|)$ and space $O(\min\{|E|,|Q|^2\})$.

A quotient path $a_0,\ldots,a_k$ can be lifted when each quotient edge comes with a witness $u_i\to_Ev_i$. Starting from a representative in the first fiber, find an internal path to $u_i$, cross the witness edge, and continue from $v_i$. An optional final internal path reaches a prescribed endpoint. Independent breadth-first searches give worst-case time $O(k(|V|+|E|))$; precomputed routing inside fibers improves repeated queries.

All fiber characters are computed by initializing one accumulator per label and, for each vertex $x$, adding $w(x)$ to the accumulator for $q(x)$. This costs $O(|V|)$ additions and $O(|Q|)$ storage. Repeating with $r$ or aggregating directly with $r\circ q$ gives the same final values.

\section{Examples and failure modes}

\begin{example}
Let $V=\{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$. Within each pair $\{0,1\}$, $\{2,3\}$, and $\{4,5\}$ include edges in both directions; also include $1\to2$ and $3\to4$. Contract the pairs to $A,B,C$. The quotient contains $A\to B\to C$, and every fiber is directed-connected. From $0$, a lift of the quotient route from $A$ to $C$ is
\[
0\to1\to2\to3\to4.
\]
Assign weights $1,2,4,8,16,32$. The fiber characters are $3,12,48$, summing to $63$. If $A,B$ are grouped into $X$ and $C$ into $Y$, the final characters are $15$ and $48$, whether computed directly or in two stages.
\end{example}

\begin{example}[Why undirected connectivity is insufficient]
Let $u,v$ share a fiber $A$, let $z$ lie in fiber $B$, and include $v\to z$. The quotient has $A\to B$. Unless $u\reach{E}v$, however, the quotient edge cannot be lifted starting at $u$. Adding $v\to u$ makes the fiber connected after directions are forgotten but still does not create the needed path from $u$ to $v$.
\end{example}

\section{Application to crystal skeletons}

Take $V$ to be a crystal graph. A first map groups vertices into quasicrystals, and a second map groups quasicrystals into larger skeleton tiles. The contraction identity says that the final edge relation is direct contraction by the composite grouping. If connectivity holds at both levels, the Two-Stage Reachability Theorem identifies final reachability with original crystal reachability.

Let $w(x)$ be the monomial weight of a crystal vertex. If first-stage fiber characters are identified with Gessel fundamental quasisymmetric functions, the Fiber-Character Associativity Theorem expresses a second-stage tile character as the sum of its fundamental characters. Once that sum is identified with a Young quasisymmetric Schur function, the total-character corollary decomposes the whole crystal character into tile characters.

The universal calculus does not itself establish these tableau-specific identifications. One must still define crystal operators and descent compositions, prove the quasicrystal character formula, identify which edges cross tile boundaries, and show that final labels and edges coincide with the elements and covers of the desired Bruhat order. For Stanley symmetric functions, reduced words and Coxeter--Knuth moves must additionally be related to this hierarchy.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The graph and character results use distinct assumptions. Exact path lifting requires directed fiber connectivity; character aggregation requires only finiteness and commutative addition. Contraction of edge relations is associative without any connectivity at all. Connectivity enters precisely when transitive closures are compared.

The all-pairs fiber condition is sufficient but may be stronger than necessary. A fixed quotient path only requires an entry representative to reach an appropriate outgoing-edge representative. A minimal interface condition could therefore replace full strong connectivity, though it would be less uniform and harder to iterate.

Concrete future work includes:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item defining semistandard Young tableaux, crystal operators, descent compositions, and quasicrystal equivalence;
\item proving Gessel fundamental characters for quasicrystal fibers and Young quasisymmetric Schur characters for skeleton tiles;
\item characterizing boundary-crossing edges and identifying the final quotient with weak or strong Bruhat covers;
\item developing reduced words, Coxeter--Knuth moves, and Stanley symmetric functions to derive their Schur expansion;
\item weakening the path-lifting hypotheses to a minimal interface condition; and
\item classifying converses and small counterexamples, particularly those demonstrating the failure of undirected connectivity.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Hierarchical contraction preserves both structural and enumerative information under transparent hypotheses. Directed fiber connectivity makes every coarse route realizable at fine scale, yielding exact reachability, representative independence, iterated lifting, and descent of antisymmetry. Fiberwise addition partitions the total character and remains associative through repeated grouping. Together, these principles form a reusable foundation for compressing crystals into quasicrystals and Young-quasisymmetric tiles while retaining control of paths and characters, and they isolate the specialized steps needed to reach Bruhat orders and Stanley symmetric functions.

